.TH FXSOUND 1 "2026-09-27" "fxsound 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fxsound \- system-wide audio enhancement for PipeWire
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fxsound
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.br
.B fxsound
.B \-\-status
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B fxsound
.B \-\-watch
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-meters ]
.br
.B fxsound
.B \-\-self\-test
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B fxsound
.B \-\-list\-apps
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fxsound
presents itself to PipeWire as a virtual sink, processes everything
applications play into it \(en a ten-band equalizer plus fidelity, ambience,
surround, dynamic boost and bass \(en and forwards the result to the real
output device. A second, independent lane does the same for a microphone: it
runs a voice chain (denoise, high-pass, downward expander, equalizer,
de-esser, compressor, makeup gain, look-ahead limiter) and publishes a virtual
source. Both lanes can run at once; each has a device and a preset of its
own, either can be detached, and one power switch covers both. An application
can have a preset of its own on either lane: FxSound runs that preset for it
alone, beside the lane's, and remembers the choice.
.PP
Each device remembers the preset it was last used with, in both directions: a
playback device its
.I .fac
preset, a microphone its voice preset. Whenever a lane moves to a device again
\(en picked in the window or the tray, named by
.BR \-\-output " or " \-\-input ,
stepped to by
.BR \-\-next\-output " or " \-\-next\-input ,
or chosen by the priority list when it is plugged in, or, with Settings \(->
Audio's
.I Follow the system's default device
on, picked in the desktop's sound settings, which FxSound then follows even
after a device was picked in FxSound \(en the device brings its
preset back; a device used for the first time leaves the preset alone.
Settings \(-> Audio sets the preset of each playback device, and Settings \(->
Microphone the voice preset of each microphone, in the device's row of its
priority list; set on the device a lane is on, it takes over at once. A renamed
preset takes its devices with it; a device whose preset was deleted keeps the
preset the lane is running when it comes back.
.PP
Run with no options,
.B fxsound
starts and shows its window, unless it was last quit with its window hidden to
the tray (see
.BR \-\-run_minimized ).
If an instance is already running, the second
process does not start a second instance: it forwards its command line to the
first over a control socket in
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/fxsound
and exits. Every option below therefore works both as a startup option and as
a way to drive an instance that is already running. A start carries out the
band lists, the effects and the preset commands too, on the preset it
selects, where the Windows build drops them at a start without a word. The one
exception is
.BR \-\-next\-output " and " \-\-next\-input :
they step a lane from the device it is on, which a start does not have yet, so
with no FxSound running such a command line is refused and nothing starts.
.PP
Only what asks for the window brings it up: a command line with no options, as
a launcher runs it,
.BR \-\-show ,
and
.B \-\-view
for a running instance.
Every option that sets something \(en a preset, the power, a device, an effect,
a band, a level, the language \(en does it without raising the window, so a
keybinding that runs
.B fxsound \-\-preset=Gaming
leaves the game in front; add
.B \-\-show
to have the window too. When such a command line is what starts FxSound, it
starts in the tray; with no tray icon after a few seconds (GNOME without an
AppIndicator extension) the window comes up minimised instead, so that FxSound
is not left running with nothing on screen. At a start,
.B \-\-view
sets the layout and the window follows the remembered tray state, unless
.B \-\-show
is given too. The Windows build raises its window for every option but
.BR \-\-status ,
and so does this one at \(lqLike FxSound for Windows\(rq = interface and
above
.RB ( \-\-windows\-parity )
for the options the Windows build has, and a start from one of them shows the
window unless FxSound was last quit hidden. The keybinding options
.RB ( \-\-toggle\-power ", " \-\-next\-preset ", " \-\-prev\-preset ", " \-\-next\-output ),
the options only this port has and D-Bus never raise it.
.BR \-\-status ", " \-\-watch " and " \-\-list\-apps
print and exit without touching the window, and
.B \-\-self\-test
never contacts a running instance at all.
.PP
This forwarding is what makes keyboard shortcuts possible. A Wayland client
cannot grab global shortcuts, by design, so the compositor owns the binding and
calls
.B fxsound
with the matching option; see
.B EXAMPLES
below. A status bar or a compositor script that would rather not spawn a
process per query can use the D-Bus interface instead; see
.BR D\-BUS .
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Processing
.TP
.BI \-\-power= 0|1|toggle
Turn audio processing on or off, for both lanes. Off also hands the session's
default output and input back to the real devices, so applications play and
record past FxSound until it is turned on again, which takes them back; an
application with a preset of its own is moved back onto it then.
Any non-zero integer means on;
.B toggle
inverts the current state.
.TP
.BI \-\-set_effect= NAME:VALUE[,NAME:VALUE...]
Set effect levels on the slider scale,
.RB 0 \(en 10 ,
as the window's sliders read them: Dynamic Boost's and Ambience's ten
positions run over the values that sound different, so a position means the
same on the command line as in the window.
Accepted names are
.BR fidelity " (also " clarity "), " ambience ", " surround ", "
.BR dynamicboost " (also " dynamic_boost ") and " bass " (also"
.BR bassboost ", " bass_boost ).
At most five pairs, since there are five effects. The music effects apply to
the output lane only, whichever lane is being edited; a microphone uses the
voice chain instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-num_bands= N
Number of equalizer bands. Only 5, 10, 15, 20 and 31 are accepted; anything
else is an error rather than a silent fallback. The curve is carried over to
the new count by frequency rather than reset, and every output preset is then
fitted onto this count: a ten-band preset picked on 31 bands stays on 31. On
the speakers the count is a setting, not an edit to the selected preset.
With
.B \-\-edit=input
it sets the voice preset's band count, and the speakers' setting is left as it
is.
.TP
.BI \-\-set_band_freq= BAND:HZ[,BAND:HZ...]
Move band centre frequencies. Band indices are 0-based. The outer bound is
20\(en20000 Hz; each band also has its own range, the one its wheel in the
window turns over, which
.B \-\-status \-\-json
reports as each band's
.BR min_frequency " and " max_frequency .
A frequency outside its band's range refuses the whole list, as a band the
equalizer does not have does (below), where the Windows build drops that pair
without a word.
.TP
.BI \-\-set_band_gain= BAND:DB[,BAND:DB...]
Set band gains in dB, 0-based band indices, gain
.RB \-12 \(en+12 .
.PP
A band list that names a band the equalizer does not have is refused whole:
nothing on it is set, and the command fails with the bands that are not there
on standard error, as
.B \-\-set_band_gain=3:4,12:2
does on ten bands. A
.B \-\-num_bands
on the same command line sets the count first. The Windows build drops such a
list, or sets what it can of it, without a word. At \(lqLike FxSound for
Windows\(rq = interface and above, either list sets the pairs that fit and
skips the others, naming them in a note on standard error, and succeeds.
.TP
.BI \-\-filter_q= Q
Equalizer filter Q,
.RB 1.0 \(en 3.0 ,
rounded to the nearest 0.5 \(en the step size of the equivalent slider.
With
.B \-\-edit=input
it sets the voice preset's own Q, which saving the preset keeps.
.TP
.BI \-\-balance= DB
Stereo balance,
.RB \-20 \(en+20
dB, rounded to a whole number. Positive pans right. Output lane only,
whichever lane is being edited.
.TP
.BI \-\-master_gain= DB
Master gain,
.RB \-20 \(en+20
dB, rounded to a whole number.
.TP
.BI \-\-volume_leveling= AMOUNT
Volume levelling amount,
.RB 0.0 \(en 4.0 ,
rounded to the nearest 0.5. Output lane only, whichever lane is being edited.
The single-l American spelling is the Windows build's and is kept verbatim.
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-suppression= LEVEL
Noise suppression for the microphone lane, as a control surface over RNNoise:
.BR off ", " light " (also " mild ", as the window calls it), " medium " or " strong ,
or
.B preset
to follow whatever the selected voice preset asks for, which is the default.
The setting outlives the preset: pick a level here and every voice preset runs
at it until it is set back to
.BR preset .
.SS Presets
One of these per command line, and of
.B \-\-next\-preset
and
.BR \-\-prev\-preset :
a second one is an error (exit status 2) that names both, where the Windows
build took the first and dropped the rest without a word. So is an empty name,
and a new name that has nothing left once the characters a preset name cannot
hold are taken out (see below).
.TP
.BI \-\-preset= NAME
Select a preset by its exact, case-sensitive name, in the edit direction's
list: a
.I .fac
preset for the output lane, a voice preset for the input lane. An unknown name
is an error, not a silent no-op; a name only the other lane has is refused with
a note saying which
.B \-\-edit
reaches it. A device named on the same command line is selected first, so
.B \-\-input=...\& \-\-preset=...
finds the preset in the microphone's list. Naming the preset that is already
selected changes nothing, as picking it again in the window does, and keeps
its unsaved changes;
.B \-\-undo_preset
reads it back from its file.
.TP
.BI \-\-save_preset= NAME
Save the current settings as a new user preset: its unsaved changes, or a copy
of it when it has none. Refused with a name a preset already has (in any
case), and at the user-preset limit
.RB ( max_user_presets
in the settings file, 10 to 1000). At \(lqLike FxSound for Windows\(rq =
interface and above a preset with no unsaved changes is refused, as on
Windows, and the menu's Save New Preset waits for changes too, while Export
Presets and Import Presets wait for none.
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite_preset
Overwrite the selected user preset with its unsaved changes. A factory preset
is refused: save its changes as a new preset instead.
.TP
.B \-\-undo_preset
Discard the selected preset's unsaved changes.
.TP
.BI \-\-rename_preset= NAME
Rename the selected user preset. A factory preset, one with unsaved changes
(save or undo them first) and a name another preset has are refused; a new
spelling of the preset's own name, such as
.B Rock
for
.BR rock ,
is a rename.
.TP
.B \-\-delete_preset
Delete the selected user preset: its file goes to the desktop's trash
.RI ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash ,
or for a preset on another filesystem that filesystem's own,
.I .Trash/UID
or
.I .Trash\-UID
at the top of it), where a file manager can restore it, or stays beside
itself as
.I NAME.fac.1.bak
(or the next free number) when no trash can take it. Its
unsaved changes go the same way from
.IR AutoSave/ ;
restore both and the preset is back with them. A factory preset is refused.
.PP
Each of these is refused exactly where the window's menu greys its item out,
with the reason on standard error. Like the window's preset list, its menu and
the tray's preset menus, they also run while processing is off.
.PP
A preset name becomes a
.I .fac
filename, and preset files are meant to travel between Linux and Windows, so
the Windows reserved characters
.RB ( < " " > " " : " " \(dq " " / " " \e " " | " " ? " " * )
are stripped from a name, each run of control characters (a line break, a
tab) becomes one space, and the result is truncated to 64 characters. A new
name is also cut to 126 bytes, on a character boundary, since a Windows FxSound
reads a preset's name in that many: 63 Cyrillic letters. Voice presets follow
the same rule with a
.I .toml
extension. A user preset saved over is written to the file it was listed
from, whatever that file is called, such as one 0.3.0 saved under the name as
written; its unsaved edits 0.3.0 kept in
.I AutoSave/
under that name are found there too.
.SS Devices and directions
.TP
.BI \-\-output= DEVICE|off
Select the playback device the output lane renders to, by its exact name. The
name is the PipeWire
.IR node.description ;
.I node.name
is accepted too, because that is what the settings file persists.
.B off
detaches the lane: FxSound hands the session default back and removes that
lane's nodes until a device is chosen again. Picking a device also makes that
lane the edit direction. For compatibility with 0.3.0, naming a microphone here is treated as
.B \-\-input
with a note on standard error.
A name that matches no device is an error, except before PipeWire has listed
the devices, as at login: then the name is held, the lane becomes the edit
direction at once, and the device is selected when the list arrives, keeping a
preset picked for it in the meantime.
A later
.BR \-\-output ,
.B off
or
.B \-\-next\-output
replaces a held name.
.TP
.BI \-\-input= DEVICE|off
The same for the microphone lane: select the capture device the voice chain
reads from, or detach the lane with
.BR off .
A playback device named here is an error rather than a guess.
.TP
.BI \-\-forget\-device= DEVICE
Forget a device that is not connected: drop it from its lane's device priority
list (Settings \(-> Audio, Settings \(-> Microphone), and the preset remembered
for it, as the \(mu beside its row there does. By
.I node.name
\(en that entry alone \(en or by description, which forgets every entry of
either lane that has it, a dock's speakers and microphone alike.
.B \-\-status \-\-json
lists the entries under
.BR output_device_list " and " input_device_list ,
with whether each is
.BR present .
A device that is connected is refused, since FxSound would learn it again at
once. An FxSound that has only just started (the bus may have started it for
.BR ForgetDevice )
holds the command for up to three seconds until PipeWire has listed the
devices, and refuses it only if no list comes.
With no FxSound running, the device is forgotten from the settings file and
nothing starts, and the exit status says whether it was: nothing is attached
to any device then, so none counts as connected. Beside options that start
FxSound, it is done the same way before they are, and beside
.B \-\-quit
too, as a running FxSound forgets the device before it quits. It cannot be
combined with
.BR \-\-status ", " \-\-watch ", " \-\-self\-test " or " \-\-list\-apps ,
which run alone; such a command line is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-edit= output|input
Choose the edit direction: the lane the window shows and the preset and effect
options above act on. The window's device combo and the tray menu
change it too, as does
.BR \-\-output " or " \-\-input
on the same command line, which
.B \-\-edit
overrides. Without either, a forwarded command line acts on whichever direction
the instance is already editing.
.TP
.BI \-\-view= 1|2
Switch the window between the Lite (1) and Pro (2) layouts. A running
instance shows its window too; at a start, the window follows the remembered
tray state (see
.BR \-\-run_minimized )
unless
.B \-\-show
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-language= CODE|system
Display language:
.B system
to follow the desktop session (the default), or one of
.BR ar ", " ba ", " bg ", " cs ", " de ", " en ", " es ", " fa ", " fi ", "
.BR fr ", " hr ", " id ", " it ", " ja ", " ko ", " nl ", " no ", " pl ", "
.BR pt ", " pt\-br ", " ro ", " ru ", " sl ", " sv ", " th ", " tr ", " ua ", "
.BR vi ", " zh\-CN " and " zh\-TW ,
in any case. The ISO codes the Windows build spells otherwise are taken too
.RB ( uk " for " ua ", " bs " for " ba ", " nb " and " nn " for " no ),
and so is a locale such as
.BR ru_RU.UTF\-8 .
A language FxSound has no translation for is an error, not a setting that
quietly shows another language.
.TP
.BI \-\-windows\-parity= off|interface|sound
\(lqLike FxSound for Windows\(rq, the slider in Settings \(-> Experimental,
saved like it:
.B off
(the default) is FxSound for Linux as it is;
.B interface
brings the window and its controls back to the Windows build's \(em the 2 dB
steps of Master Gain and Balance, its readouts, tooltips and light theme, the
equalizer's straight-line curve and one-way end wheels, the language order,
and with the power off no preset picked from the window, its menu or the tray
(the command line and D-Bus still pick them) \(em without changing the sound;
.B sound
adds the Windows build's sound, so that a preset sounds as it does there.
Each level includes the ones before it, none of them changes or deletes
another setting, and
.B off
brings everything back as it was. Some parts of the levels are still being
built; the level is saved and reported
.RB ( windows_parity
in
.BR \-\-status )
already.
The fourth level,
.B full
.RB ( everything ),
which hides and stops what the Windows build does not have, comes in a later
version: this one refuses it with exit status 1, with or without
.BR \-\-force ,
and runs a
.I settings.toml
that says
.B full
as
.BR sound ,
keeping
.B full
in the file for the version that offers it.
.TP
.B \-\-force
With
.BR \-\-windows\-parity=full ,
in the version that offers it: switch even when that takes something in use
away. Refused along with
.B full
in this one.
.TP
.BR \-\-run_minimized ", " \-\-hide
Start without showing the window, or hide the window of a running instance.
This is what the autostart entry invokes. Without either, a start shows the
window unless FxSound was last quit with its window hidden to the tray, as the
Windows build does; such a start with no tray icon to be in after a few seconds
(GNOME without an AppIndicator extension) brings the window up minimised.
.TP
.B \-\-activated
Start without showing the window, as
.B \-\-hide
does; but when FxSound is already running, exit at once with status 0 and
forward nothing. This is what the systemd user unit and the D-Bus activation
file invoke, so that a bus call arriving while an instance started from the
launcher is still starting does not hide its window.
.TP
.B \-\-show
Show and raise the window of the running instance \(en a minimised one
included \(en or, starting FxSound, show the window even though it was in the
tray when FxSound was last quit.
.SS Applications
.PP
Every application that plays or records through FxSound is remembered, and
each can have a preset of its own per lane: a game on
.IR Gaming ,
a browser on
.IR "Volume Boost" ,
a voice chat's microphone on
.IR Headset ,
all at once. FxSound moves the application's streams onto nodes of their own
on the lane's device that run that preset, as
.BR pavucontrol (1)
moves a stream, and moves them back when the preset is taken away; the rest
of the session keeps the lane's preset. With the power off none of them is
moved: they play and record through the system's default devices like every
other application until the power is turned on again, and one that holds on to
its preset's node by itself stays there unprocessed. At most four presets of
one lane run this way at a time. Settings \(-> Applications offers the same choices. None
of these options raises the window.
.TP
.BI \-\-app\-preset= APP=PRESET
Give an application a playback preset of its own.
.I APP
is a remembered application's Flatpak id, program or name, in any case, as
.B \-\-list\-apps
shows them: the first of the three that any remembered application has
decides, and every application it names gets the preset, so
.B firefox
reaches both a native and a Flatpak Firefox. One playing or recording now is
reached by its own as well, even while a rule written for another install
covers it. One that FxSound has not seen gets a rule for what
.I APP
looks like, which applies from the first time it plays: a Flatpak id for
three or more parts between dots, such as
.BR com.discordapp.Discord ;
the name an application gives itself for text with a space, such as
.BR "Battlefield 6" ;
a program for anything else, such as
.B bf6.exe
or
.BR firefox .
A note on standard error says which, as a misspelt name would be new too.
Given as FxSound starts, such a name waits up to three seconds for FxSound to
hear which applications play and record, so one already playing is reached
by its own name.
.I PRESET
is an output preset's exact name, or
.BR default " (also " follow ,
in any case, or nothing after the
.BR = )
to have the application follow the output lane's preset again. A preset the
output lane does not have is an error. May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-app\-input\-preset= APP=PRESET
The same for what an application records, with a voice preset.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-apps
Print the applications FxSound remembers and exit: the ones playing or
recording now first, then the rest, the most recently heard first. One line
per application of
.I key=value
pairs, spelled as
.B \-\-watch
spells them:
.BR name " (the one the window shows), " binary ", " flatpak ", " running ,
.BR output_preset " and " input_preset
(empty while the application follows the lane),
.BR routed.output " and " routed.input
(the preset FxSound runs it through now, empty on the lane's own). With
.BR \-\-json ,
one object:
.B schema
and
.BR apps ,
described under
.BR "STATUS DOCUMENT" .
With no FxSound running, the list is read from the store,
.IR ~/.config/fxsound/apps.toml ,
nothing in it running, and nothing is started. Every other option on the
same command line is ignored.
.SS Shortcut targets
.PP
These exist so a compositor keybinding has something to call. They have no
Windows counterpart, where the application registers global hotkeys itself.
.TP
.B \-\-toggle\-power
Toggle audio processing, as
.B \-\-power=toggle
does.
.TP
.B \-\-toggle\-window
Show the window if it is hidden, hide it if it is showing \(en the same toggle
a left click on the tray icon performs. Starting FxSound, it shows the window,
as
.B \-\-show
does.
.TP
.B \-\-next\-preset
Select the next preset of the edit direction, wrapping.
.TP
.B \-\-prev\-preset
Select the previous preset of the edit direction, wrapping.
.TP
.B \-\-next\-output
Cycle the output lane to the next usable playback device, wrapping, in the
order of the device priority list (Settings \(-> Audio). Playback devices only:
it never crosses into the microphones. With no FxSound running there is no
device to step from: the command line is refused (exit status 1) and nothing
on it is done or started;
.BI \-\-output= NAME
starts FxSound on a device. An FxSound that has only just started (the bus
may have started it for
.BR NextOutput )
holds the command for up to three seconds until PipeWire has listed the
devices, and steps then.
.TP
.B \-\-next\-input
Cycle the microphone lane to the next usable capture device, wrapping, in the
order of the microphones' priority list (Settings \(-> Microphone). Capture
devices only. With no FxSound running it is refused as
.B \-\-next\-output
is, and an FxSound that has only just started waits for the device list as
.B \-\-next\-output
does.
.SS Control
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print the running instance's state on standard output and exit, one
.I key: value
line per item: the version, whether processing is on, then
.BR output ", " input ", " output_preset ", " input_preset " and " direction
(the edit direction), the lines 0.3.0 printed, and last
.BR noise_suppression ", " echo_cancel
and the voice chain's telemetry as
.B input_
lines, with
.B windows_parity
after
.BR theme .
Every other option on the same command line is ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the
.BR \-\-status ", " \-\-watch ", " \-\-self\-test " or " \-\-list\-apps
report as JSON instead of as lines: one object for
.B \-\-status
(schema 3, described under
.BR "STATUS DOCUMENT" ),
for
.B \-\-self\-test
.RB ( version ", " checks ", " ok )
and for
.B \-\-list\-apps
.RB ( schema " and " apps ),
one object per line for
.BR \-\-watch .
Not the default: the line format is what existing scripts already grep.
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Subscribe to the running instance and print one event per line until it
quits or standard output is closed. The instance's last event before it quits
is
.BR quit ,
and only then does
.B \-\-watch
exit 0; a stream that ends without it \(en the instance dropped a reader
that fell too far behind, or it died \(en exits 1 with
.I the event stream was cut off
on standard error, so a loop that restarts
.B \-\-watch
until it succeeds keeps it running. The first event is
.B status
(the full
.B \-\-status
document); after that
.BR power ", " preset_changed ", " device_changed ", " devices_changed ", "
.BR audio_state ", " direction ", " notice ", " echo_cancel ", " calibrated ", "
.BR app_routed ", " windows_parity ", " window " and " quit
as they happen;
.B windows_parity
carries the new
.BR level ;
.B app_routed
says where an application's streams went
.RB ( app ", " binary ", " flatpak ", " direction ", " preset ,
the preset being empty once they are back on the lane's own chain). Plain output is
.I event key=value ...
per line; with
.B \-\-json
each line is one object carrying
.BR \(dqv\(dq:1 ", " \(dqevent\(dq " and " \(dqts\(dq
(milliseconds since the epoch) plus the event's fields. This is what a status
bar module should use instead of polling
.BR \-\-status .
.TP
.B \-\-meters
With
.BR \-\-watch ,
also stream the microphone lane's meters as
.B input_meters
events, at most four per second. Off by default because a bar that only wants
to know the preset name should not be woken that often.
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Check the installation and exit: the version; that the factory
.I .fac
presets and the voice presets are found under the install prefix and parse;
that the settings file parses; an offline run of both processing chains with
RNNoise instantiated; that the desktop entry, the icons (application and
status), the systemd unit, the D-Bus activation file, this manual page and the
AppStream metainfo are present, and that the unit and the activation file
start this binary; that the runtime directory can be created. Run from a source tree,
the install-only files are reported as
.BR skip .
PipeWire and the session bus are reported as
.B skip
rather than as failures when there is none, so the test is meaningful in a
container with no session; when they are there, it only checks that something
answers on their sockets. It writes nothing, not even a broken settings file
aside. One
.I name: ok|skip|fail detail
line per check. It runs before the single-instance lock is taken,
so it works beside a running FxSound and never forwards anything to it. The
exit status is 0 if and only if every check that was not skipped passed.
.TP
.B \-\-quit
Quit the running instance, autosaving a modified preset first.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Options whose Windows spelling uses underscores also accept the hyphenated
form:
.B \-\-num\-bands
and
.B \-\-num_bands
are the same option. The hyphenated spellings are hidden from
.BR \-\-help .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For a forwarded command line, this means the running instance accepted
it, not that the effect is audible yet. For
.BR \-\-self\-test ,
every check that ran passed.
.TP
.B 1
The command failed and standard error or the report says why: the running
instance refused it (an unknown preset or device name, a factory preset to
overwrite, a band the equalizer does not have, a device to forget that is
connected, or
.BR \-\-windows\-parity=full ,
which comes in a later version, for example) or could
not be reached, there is no running instance for
.BR \-\-status " or " \-\-watch
to ask or for
.BR \-\-next\-output " or " \-\-next\-input
to step, the
.B \-\-watch
stream was cut off without the instance's
.B quit
event,
.B \-\-self\-test
found at least one failing check,
.B \-\-list\-apps
with no FxSound running found a store it could not read,
.B \-\-forget\-device
with no FxSound running (on its own or beside
.BR \-\-quit )
found no such device or a settings file it could not read or write, or FxSound
itself could not start.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed \(en an unknown option, a value outside
its range, an unknown language, two preset options, an empty preset name, an
empty device name for
.BR \-\-forget\-device ,
or
.B \-\-forget\-device
beside
.BR \-\-status ", " \-\-watch ", " \-\-self\-test " or " \-\-list\-apps .
.SH STATUS DOCUMENT
.B \-\-status \-\-json
prints one JSON object. D-Bus
.B GetStatus
answers with the same object and
.B \-\-watch
opens with it. It carries every key 0.3.0 printed, with the same meaning,
and every key the Windows build's
.B \-\-status
writes (marked
.IR upstream ),
with that build's meaning and JSON type, so a client written for either reads
it. Keys are only ever added; a key removed, renamed or given another meaning
would change
.B schema
(see
.B Compatibility
below).
The equalizer and
.BR preset ", " presets " and " selected_preset
are the edit direction's: the lane the window shows. The effects,
.BR balance_db " and " volume_leveling
exist on the output lane only and are always the output lane's.
.TP
.B schema
3, the compatibility number described under
.BR Compatibility .
The 0.3.0 document had no number and is schema 1; 0.4.0's is 2, and 3 keeps
every key of 2.
.TP
.BR version ", " power
.RI ( upstream )
The version, and whether processing is on.
.TP
.BR audio ", " preset ", " device ", " direction ", " edit_direction ", " view ", " theme
The edit direction's lane
.RB ( processing ", " idle " or " unavailable ),
its preset with
.B *
for unsaved changes, its device by description, the edit direction
.RB ( output " or " input ,
under both names), and the window's view and theme.
.TP
.B effects
.BR fidelity ", " ambience ", " surround ", " dynamic_boost " and " bass
on their 0\(en10 scale, and
.RI ( upstream )
.BR clarity " and " dynamicboost ,
the same values as
.BR fidelity " and " dynamic_boost .
Exact: a whole value is written as an integer, as 0.3.0 wrote every one, and
any other with its fraction.
.TP
.BR master_gain_db ", " balance_db ", " volume_leveling ", " filter_q ", " eq
The gain stage, and
.BR eq :
.BR enabled ", " bands " and " max_bands .
.TP
.BR format ", " ring
The negotiated
.BR sample_rate " and " channels ,
and the ring between the two audio nodes since its stream was built:
.BR dropped_frames ", " underrun_frames ", " resyncs " and " format_mismatches .
.TP
.BR output ", " input
One object per lane:
.BR enabled ", " device ", " node_name ", " preset " (without " * "), " modified
and
.BR active ;
.B input
adds
.BR noise_suppression ", " denoise_level " and " hidden ,
whether \(lqLike FxSound for Windows\(rq at
.B full
hides the microphone lane (never in this version).
A detached lane has
.B null
for its device.
.TP
.BR input_meters ", " echo_cancel
The microphone's telemetry
.RB ( voice_probability ,
0 while the denoiser is not running;
.BR noise_floor_db ,
.B null
until measured; the
.B _reduction_db
of the denoiser, gate, compressor and de-esser, the denoiser's 0 while it is
not running; and whether the denoiser and
the de-esser are running), and the echo canceller's
.BR on " and " running .
.TP
.B presets
.RI ( upstream )
.B built_in
and
.BR user_defined :
arrays of
.BR name " and " modified ,
in the order the preset list shows them.
.BR output_presets " and " input_presets
are the same for each lane, whichever the window shows.
.TP
.B selected_preset
.RI ( upstream )
The preset by the name
.B \-\-preset
takes, or
.B null
while the lane has none.
.TP
.BR output_devices ", " input_devices
Arrays of names: the description of each device PipeWire has now, in the
order of
.BR output_device_list " and " input_device_list .
.B output_devices
is
.IR upstream .
.TP
.BR output_device_list ", " input_device_list
Arrays of
.BR node_name ", " description " and " present :
every device the lane's priority list knows, in its order, then any it has
not learnt yet;
.B present
is whether PipeWire has it now, and a device that is not keeps the
description it was last seen with.
.TP
.BR selected_output ", " selected_input
Each lane's device by description, or
.B null
while the lane is detached.
.B selected_output
is
.IR upstream .
.TP
.B equalizer
.RI ( upstream )
.BR num_bands ", " master_gain " (dB), " volume_leveling ", " filter_q ,
.BR balance " (dB) and " bands :
an array of
.BR index ", " frequency " (Hz), " gain " (dB), " min_frequency " and " max_frequency ,
the range the window's wheel tunes the band's frequency over, which the
Windows build leaves out. It is that build's wheel range, except that the first
and last band reach half a band past the ladder's ends (46 Hz and 20 kHz on ten
bands, where the Windows build stops at 62.5 Hz and 16 kHz).
.TP
.B apps
Every application FxSound remembers, in the order
.B \-\-list\-apps
prints them: an array of
.B name
(the one the window shows, which
.B \-\-app\-preset
takes back),
.B binary
and
.B flatpak
(empty when PipeWire did not say),
.B running
(playing or recording now),
.BR output_preset " and " input_preset
(the preset of its own for each lane, as chosen, or
.B null
while it follows the lane) and
.BR routed :
an object of
.BR output " and " input ,
the preset FxSound has moved its streams onto in each lane, or
.B null
while they run through the lane's own chain \(en it follows the lane, it is
not playing or recording there, or its preset could not be run.
.TP
.BR windows_parity ", " apps_hidden
The level of \(lqLike FxSound for Windows\(rq
.RB ( off ", " interface " or " sound ;
.B full
from the later version that offers it),
and whether it hides the applications' presets, so that a status bar can grey
its microphone and application parts instead of failing; always
.B false
in this version.
.SS Compatibility
The JSON FxSound prints \(en this document and D-Bus
.BR GetStatus ,
the
.BR ListPresets ", " ListDevices " and " ListApps
answers and
.B \-\-list\-apps \-\-json
(which carry this document's
.BR schema ),
and the events of
.B \-\-watch \-\-json
(which carry
.BR \(dqv\(dq )
\(en follows one rule.
.B schema
and
.B v
are compatibility numbers, not versions. Adding a key or an event never
changes them, so a later FxSound may print keys this page does not list, and a
client ignores what it does not know. Removing or
renaming a key, or changing its meaning or its JSON type, raises the number by
one; where a meaning has to change, the new meaning gets a key of its own and
the old key keeps the old one, as
.B edit_direction
did beside
.BR direction .
A client written for schema 3 reads any document that says 3, and one that
needs a key added later looks for the key rather than at the number.
.B schema
went from 1 to 2 and to 3 with keys that 0.4.0 and 0.5.0 added, before the
rule was written; it stays 3 while keys are only added.
.B \-\-self\-test \-\-json
carries no number and follows the same rule: its
.BR version ", " checks " (" name ", " status ", " detail ") and " ok
keep their meaning, new checks may appear, and
.B detail
is words for a person, not for parsing.
.SH D\-BUS
A running instance also serves an interface on the session bus, for status
bars, compositor scripts and anything else that would rather hold a connection
than spawn a process per query. The command line stays on the control socket
so that it keeps working without a session bus; both paths run exactly the
same commands, so they cannot disagree.
.TP
Bus names
.B org.fxsound.FxSound
(the API) and
.B com.fxsound.FxSound
(the desktop-entry id, so a shell can associate the window with it).
.TP
Object path
.I /org/fxsound/FxSound
.TP
Interface
.B org.fxsound.FxSound
.PP
Methods:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
TogglePower() \(-> b          SetPower(b)
NextPreset()               PrevPreset()
SetPreset(s)               GetPreset() \(-> s
SetOutput(s)               SetInput(s)
NextOutput()               NextInput()
SetNoiseSuppression(s)     SetEditDirection(s)
GetStatus() \(-> s            (the \-\-status \-\-json document)
Show()                     Hide()
ToggleWindow()             Quit()
Apply(as argv) \(-> (b ok, s stdout, s stderr)
ListPresets() \(-> s          ListDevices() \(-> s
SetAppPreset(s app, s direction, s preset)
ListApps() \(-> s             ForgetDevice(s)
SetWindowsParity(s level, b force)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Each method does what the matching option does \(en
.B TogglePower
is
.BR \-\-toggle\-power ,
.B SetPreset
is
.BR \-\-preset ,
.B SetEditDirection
is
.B \-\-edit
\(en with the same words for its argument:
.B SetOutput
and
.B SetInput
take a
.B node.name
or a description, or
.B off
to detach the lane;
.B SetEditDirection
takes
.BR output " or " input ;
.B ForgetDevice
is
.BR \-\-forget\-device .
.B SetWindowsParity
is
.BR \-\-windows\-parity ,
and
.I force
is
.BR \-\-force ;
.B full
is refused with
.B org.fxsound.FxSound.Error.Refused
in this version.
.B SetAppPreset
is
.BR \-\-app\-preset " and " \-\-app\-input\-preset :
the application as they take it, the direction as
.B SetEditDirection
takes it, and the preset by name, or
.BR default ", " follow
or the empty string to have the application follow the lane again.
Unlike a typed command line, no method raises the window but
.BR Show " and " ToggleWindow .
A command the running instance refuses fails with
.B org.fxsound.FxSound.Error.Refused
and the message
.B fxsound
would print; an argument the option would not take either, with
.BR org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs ;
a command it did not answer within four seconds, with
.BR org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.Failed .
.PP
.B Apply
runs a whole command line and answers with what
.B fxsound
given it would: whether it succeeded, and what it printed on standard output
and on standard error.
.I argv
holds the options without the program name; a leading
.B fxsound
is taken too. A line that does not parse, or that the instance refuses,
answers
.B false
with the reason rather than failing the call, and only no answer within four
seconds fails it. Like the other methods, it leaves the window alone unless
the line says
.BR \-\-show ", " \-\-toggle\-window " or " \-\-hide .
.B ListPresets
answers with the
.BR schema ", " edit_direction ", " selected_preset ", " presets ", "
.BR output_presets " and " input_presets
of the status document, and
.B ListDevices
with its
.BR schema ", " selected_output ", " selected_input ", "
.BR output_device_list " and " input_device_list ,
each as one JSON object.
.B ListApps
answers with what
.B \-\-list\-apps \-\-json
prints: the
.BR schema " and " apps
of the status document.
.PP
Properties:
.BR Version " (s, constant), " Power " (b), " Preset " (s), "
.BR Output " (s), " Input " (s), " Direction " (s) and " WindowsParity " (s)."
.B Preset
is the edit direction's preset, without the
.B *
for unsaved changes;
.B Output
and
.B Input
are the lanes' devices by description, empty while a lane is detached;
.B Direction
is the edit direction;
.B WindowsParity
is the level of \(lqLike FxSound for Windows\(rq, as
.B SetWindowsParity
takes it.
.PP
Signals:
.BR PowerChanged (b),
.BR PresetChanged (s\ direction,\ s\ name),
.BR DeviceChanged (s\ direction,\ s\ node_name,\ s\ description),
.BR AudioStateChanged (s\ json),
.BR Notice (s\ message)
and
.BR AppRouted (s\ app,\ s\ direction,\ s\ preset).
They follow the
.B \-\-watch
events
.BR power ", " preset_changed ", " device_changed ", " audio_state ", " notice
and
.BR app_routed ;
.B AppRouted
names the application as the window does, and its preset is empty once its
streams are back on the lane's own chain;
.B AudioStateChanged
carries the line
.B \-\-watch \-\-json
prints, and a preset that only gains unsaved changes sends no
.BR PresetChanged .
A name is empty for a lane that has no preset or no device.
.PP
If the name is already taken on the bus, the instance logs that and serves the
control socket only. Any call made while FxSound is not running starts it
first, in the tray, a property read included: the bus reads
.I /usr/share/dbus\-1/services/org.fxsound.FxSound.service
and hands the start to the systemd user unit,
.IR fxsound.service ,
or runs
.B fxsound \-\-activated
itself where the bus has no systemd. A
.BR NextOutput ", " NextInput " or " ForgetDevice
call that starts FxSound this way waits, up to three seconds, for PipeWire to
list the devices, and then steps or forgets as it would in an instance that
had been running; with no FxSound running,
.BR \-\-next\-output " and " \-\-next\-input
are refused instead. A started FxSound takes over the
session's default devices, so anything that only reads or polls \(en a status
bar module above all \(en must ask the bus not to start it, or it will bring
FxSound back seconds after the user quit it:
.B busctl \-\-auto\-start=no call
does, as does the message flag
.B NO_AUTO_START
in any other client. A property is read that way with
.BR org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties " " Get ,
as below:
.B busctl get\-property
and
.B busctl introspect
start FxSound whatever
.B \-\-auto\-start
says (systemd 262). At most 32 calls are answered at once; past that a call
is refused with
.BR org.fxsound.FxSound.Error.Refused ,
as a command line past the control socket's limit is. With
.BR busctl (1):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
busctl \-\-user call org.fxsound.FxSound /org/fxsound/FxSound \e
    org.fxsound.FxSound SetPreset s Music
busctl \-\-user call org.fxsound.FxSound /org/fxsound/FxSound \e
    org.fxsound.FxSound Apply as 2 \-\- \-\-preset=Music \-\-set_effect=bass:7.5
busctl \-\-user \-\-auto\-start=no call org.fxsound.FxSound /org/fxsound/FxSound \e
    org.freedesktop.DBus.Properties Get ss org.fxsound.FxSound Power
busctl \-\-user monitor org.fxsound.FxSound
.EE
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Holds the instance lock and the control socket. When the session provides none,
.IB /tmp/fxsound- uid
is used instead.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Settings live under
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/fxsound ,
defaulting to
.IR ~/.config/fxsound .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
User presets live under
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/fxsound/presets ,
defaulting to
.IR ~/.local/share/fxsound/presets .
.TP
.B DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
Where the D-Bus interface is served. Unset, the instance runs socket-only and
.B \-\-self\-test
reports the bus as skipped.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter, in the usual
.B env_logger
syntax, for example
.BR RUST_LOG=debug .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/fxsound/presets/Factsoft/
Factory presets, in the
.I .fac
format shared with the Windows build.
.TP
.I /usr/share/fxsound/presets/BonusPresets/
Bonus presets, same format.
.TP
.I /usr/share/fxsound/presets/Input/
Voice presets for the microphone chain, in TOML. They are TOML rather than
.I .fac
because the
.I .fac
format is a byte-for-byte contract with the Windows build and has nowhere to
put a gate threshold.
.PP
Only
.I /usr
and
.I /usr/local
are searched for these directories, the one the binary is installed under
first: a binary under
.I /usr/local
uses the presets installed with it even where a distribution package has left
others, perhaps an older release's, under
.IR /usr .
An install under any other prefix starts with an empty preset list.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/fxsound/presets/
Imported and user-saved presets; voice presets under
.IR Input/ .
.TP
.I ~/.config/fxsound/settings.toml
Settings. A corrupt file is never fatal: the defaults are used and the audio
still comes up. One that does not load \(en it does not parse, is not UTF-8, or
may not be read \(en is moved aside to
.I settings.toml.bad
first, or to
.I settings.toml.2.bad
and so on, never over an earlier one. The Windows keys FxSound does not use
\(en the hotkey chords, the window position, always-on-top and the update
check \(en are read from an older file without complaint and not written back.
Keys this version does not know, such as those a later version wrote, are kept
and written back as they were; 0.4.0 drops them on its next save.
This file,
.I apps.toml
and a preset may be symbolic links, as a dotfile manager leaves them: a save
replaces the file the link points to and keeps the link.
A link to a read-only file, or to one in a directory the user may not write,
is never saved through, so every save of that file fails; home-manager's
default links into
.I /nix/store
are such links, and its
.B \%mkOutOfStoreSymlink
makes one FxSound can save through.
.TP
.I ~/.config/fxsound/apps.toml
The applications FxSound remembers, at most 500, and the preset each has of
its own. Past 500, those that only follow the lanes are forgotten first, seen
longest ago first; one with a preset of its own goes only when there are more
than 500 of those. One that does not load is moved aside to
.I apps.toml.bad
(or
.I apps.toml.2.bad
and so on, never over an earlier one) when FxSound starts, and the list starts
empty.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/fxsound/instance.lock
Holds the running instance's pid under an exclusive
.BR flock (2).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/fxsound/instance.sock
The control socket. One JSON line per request, one per reply; a
.B \-\-watch
connection stays open and receives one JSON line per event.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/fxsound.service
A systemd user unit, installed but not enabled. See
.B NOTES
below.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus\-1/services/org.fxsound.FxSound.service
Starts FxSound, through that unit, when a D-Bus call arrives for it and it is
not running. See
.BR D\-BUS .
.TP
.I /usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/status/com.fxsound.FxSound\-{off,on,processing}.svg
The tray icon's three states, looked up by name by the StatusNotifier host.
The binary carries the same three and offers them as pixmaps too, for a host
that does not look icons up in the theme.
.TP
.I /usr/share/icons/hicolor/{256x256,32x32}/apps/com.fxsound.FxSound.png
The application icon under the application id, the icon name FxSound's
notifications carry; the same icon is also installed as
.IR fxsound.png ,
the name the desktop entry uses.
.SH EXAMPLES
Bind the shortcut targets in the compositor, here on Super+Alt, which few
desktops and no applications use \(en in Hyprland:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bind = SUPER ALT, P, exec, fxsound \-\-toggle\-power
bind = SUPER ALT, W, exec, fxsound \-\-toggle\-window
bind = SUPER ALT, bracketright, exec, fxsound \-\-next\-preset
bind = SUPER ALT, bracketleft, exec, fxsound \-\-prev\-preset
bind = SUPER ALT, O, exec, fxsound \-\-next\-output
.EE
.RE
.PP
and in sway:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bindsym $mod+Mod1+p exec fxsound \-\-toggle\-power
bindsym $mod+Mod1+w exec fxsound \-\-toggle\-window
bindsym $mod+Mod1+bracketright exec fxsound \-\-next\-preset
bindsym $mod+Mod1+bracketleft exec fxsound \-\-prev\-preset
bindsym $mod+Mod1+o exec fxsound \-\-next\-output
.EE
.RE
.PP
A compositor binding takes its chord from every application, so leave the
Windows build's own chords alone: Ctrl+Shift+Q, its power toggle, is how
Chromium quits, and Ctrl+Shift+E, Ctrl+Shift+A, Ctrl+Shift+Z and Ctrl+Shift+W
mean something in browsers and editors too.
.PP
A worked set of these, with the window rules, is installed at
.IR /usr/share/doc/fxsound\-linux/hyprland.conf.example
.RI ( examples/hyprland.conf.example
on Debian).
.PP
Ask a running instance what it is doing:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-status \-\-json | jq .
.EE
.RE
.PP
Feed a status bar without polling: one JSON object per line, starting with
the full status.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-watch \-\-json | jq \-\-unbuffered \-r '.event'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Point the microphone lane at a headset and quieten a fan:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-input='Jabra Evolve2' \-\-preset='Gaming Headset' \e
    \-\-noise\-suppression=strong
.EE
.RE
.PP
Give a game and a browser presets of their own, and a voice chat's
microphone a voice preset \(en by program, by program and by Flatpak id, even
before any of them has run \(en then check what runs where:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-app\-preset='bf6.exe=Gaming' \-\-app\-preset='brave=Volume Boost' \e
    \-\-app\-input\-preset='com.discordapp.Discord=Headset'
fxsound \-\-list\-apps
.EE
.RE
.PP
Select a preset and lift the bass on the speakers without opening the window:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-edit=output \-\-preset=Music \-\-set_effect=bass:7,ambience:3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check an installation, for example from a package's post-install test:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fxsound \-\-self\-test \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The window's close button, like the compositor's close request, hides the
window and keeps processing: the tray's
.BR Exit " and " \-\-quit
are the ways out. With a tray icon on screen the minimise button hides the
window into the tray as well; without one \(en GNOME without an AppIndicator
extension \(en it minimises the window, as the Windows build's does, so that
the window cannot vanish with nothing left to bring it back. Where a window was
hidden or minimised with no tray icon,
.B fxsound \-\-show
or launching FxSound again brings it back; on Wayland, where an application
cannot take its own window out of the minimised state, as a fresh window in its
place. The tray menu lists the playback
devices under
.B Playback Device Select
and the microphones under
.BR "Recording Device Select" ,
a long name shortened in its middle so that the part that tells two devices of
one card apart stays; at \(lqLike FxSound for Windows\(rq = interface and
above a playback device's name is cut after 30 characters, as the Windows build
cuts it, and with the power off the preset submenus are left out, as there.
.PP
The systemd user unit is installed but deliberately not enabled. The desktop
entry already starts FxSound when you launch it, and an enabled user unit on
top of that puts two processes in a race for the control socket; the loser
exits, but which one that is depends on the session's startup order. To have
FxSound come up with the session instead of on demand:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now fxsound
.EE
.RE
.PP
FxSound started by the user unit \(en enabled, or by a D-Bus call \(en runs in
the systemd user manager's environment, not the compositor's. Where the
compositor does not import
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
into it (sway, and Hyprland without uwsm), that FxSound has no display: it runs
in the tray, and asking for its window brings a notification that it could not
be opened instead; quit it and start it again once the variable is imported.
Import it when the compositor starts, in sway with:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
exec dbus\-update\-activation\-environment \-\-systemd WAYLAND_DISPLAY DISPLAY
.EE
.RE
.PP
and in Hyprland with the same command after
.BR exec\-once\ = .
.PP
Switching the session's default sink onto the FxSound sink is a session-manager
operation. Without
.BR wireplumber (1)
or an equivalent, the sink appears in the graph and nothing moves onto it.
.PP
The preset import and export dialogs are XDG portal calls. They need a portal
backend that implements the file chooser (the GTK, GNOME or KDE one);
wlroots' own backend does not.
